{"title":"2025 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 12","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Diagram\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagram</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Cavalieri&#39;s_Principle)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Cavalieri's Principle)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Calculus)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Calculus)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The windshield wiper on the driver's side of a large bus is depicted below.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/c/4dc68bcd27d22fb65a75bcaa7cf7dc231863514b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] /* Made by MRENTHUSIASM */ size(200); pair A, B, C, D; A = origin; B = (3/2,3*sqrt(3)/2); C = B+(0,1.75); D = B-(0,1.75); draw(A--B,linewidth(1.1)); draw(C--D,red+linewidth(2.1)); dot(A^^B,linewidth(5)); label(&quot;$A$&quot;,A,1.5*W); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,1.5*E); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,C,1.5*E); label(&quot;$D$&quot;,D,1.5*E); [/asy]\" width=\"165\" height=\"335\" />\nArm <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/0/840e2b592390eb6ec918fa6f3292716ce170de66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AB}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> pivots back and forth around point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, sweeping out an arc of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/d/08d5c45388de5c75af641c046d466de3592ffa98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />, symmetric about the vertical line through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. The wiper blade <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/0/ce0bfc603f9a044e308b1a1fdadca917e81ad2dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{CD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" /> is attached to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> at its midpoint and stays vertical as the arm moves. The arm is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> feet long, and the wiper blade is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/b/87b8245e043508c7c928c89b42f9ce38f04d720d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" /> feet tall. What is the area of the windshield cleaned by the wiper, in square feet, to the nearest hundredth? (Assume that the windshield is a flat vertical surface.)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/6/486dc6b4f2d42dad16b344bbaf619b096af6c8c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 9.68 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 10.14 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } 10.50 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 11.32 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 12.00$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"508\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagram\">Diagram</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Diagram\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/e/7ee73f1ac45a68b17f1bcdab5ff29cd8e6c24fff.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] /* Made by MRENTHUSIASM */ size(200); pair A, B, C, D, B1, C1, D1; A = origin; B = (3/2,3*sqrt(3)/2); C = B+(0,1.75); D = B-(0,1.75); B1 = (-B.x,B.y); C1 = (-C.x,C.y); D1 = (-D.x,D.y); fill(C--shift(0,1.75)*Arc(A,B,B1)--D1--shift(0,-1.75)*Arc(A,3,120,60)--cycle,palered); draw(A--B,linewidth(1.1)); draw(A--B1,dashed+linewidth(1.1)); draw(C--D,red+linewidth(2.1)); draw(C1--D1,red+dashed+linewidth(2.1)); draw(Arc(A,B,B1),dashed+linewidth(1.1),Arrows(12)); draw(shift(0,1.75)*Arc(A,B,B1),red+dashed+linewidth(2.1),Arrows(12)); draw(shift(0,-1.75)*Arc(A,B,B1),red+dashed+linewidth(2.1),Arrows(12)); dot(A^^B^^B1,linewidth(5)); label(&quot;$A$&quot;,A,1.5*W); label(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,1.5*E); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,C,1.5*E); label(&quot;$D$&quot;,D,1.5*E); label(&quot;$60^{\\circ}$&quot;,A+(0,3),1.5*N); label(&quot;$3$&quot;,0.55*B,dir(-30)); Label L1 = Label(&quot;$3.5$&quot;, align=(0,0), position=MidPoint, filltype=Fill(3,3,white)); draw(C+(1,0)--D+(1,0), L=L1, arrow=Arrows(10),bar=Bars(15), linewidth(1.1)); [/asy]\" width=\"298\" height=\"335\" />\n~MRENTHUSIASM\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Cavalieri.27s_Principle.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Cavalieri's_Principle)\">Solution 1 (Cavalieri's Principle)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Cavalieri&#39;s Principle)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The area cleaned by the wiper follows a thickened curve with vertical ends, where the curve is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/f/29fa0a9949f018e121b2093216dd5db13d3abef0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> arc with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Since the sides are vertical, by Cavalieri's Principle, the area is equivalent to a rectangle with side lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/b/87b8245e043508c7c928c89b42f9ce38f04d720d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" /> and the distance between the two vertical ends. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/7/4c71f19552a05ee5e2b31e94aff3fb986884bf31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B&#39;$\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> be the result of point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> at its leftmost point after it has swept <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/f/29fa0a9949f018e121b2093216dd5db13d3abef0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/a/74a48760ced9fbfbbf84cff69d7d094425db8399.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABB&#39;$\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> is equilateral so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/1/e0179b461b3ababf06c03702d2f4c9969626b778.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BB&#39; = AB = 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"122\" height=\"16\" />. So the area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/2/f62fb48a33ef39efdd9e14ee1d409a0a428ed7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3.5 \\cdot 3 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } 10.50}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"159\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>Note: if you aren't aware of Cavalieri's Principle, you can still recognize this by slicing the top curved part of the shape and moving it to the bottom, creating a rectangle with the same area as the original shape.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Calculus.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Calculus)\">Solution 2 (Calculus)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Calculus)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>As solution 1 kind of explains, the top and bottom of the area swept out follow the exact same path shape, so we can think of the top one as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/6/c96dd6ec1dc4ad7520fbdc78fcdbec9edd068d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"37\" height=\"20\" /> and the bottom one as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/e/72ee6e50e308dfff562a0e142d6bf01497dbfce5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(x)-3.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"83\" height=\"20\" />. As solution 1 points out, the width of the figure is 3. Then, the area between them is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/2/4826e97c591686fdeb646ac2854e3291e3eee450.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_{0}^{3}[f(x)-(f(x)-3.5)]dx = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) } 10.50}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"322\" height=\"46\" />.\n</p><p>~dg6665\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Using the diagram (credit - MRENTHUSIASM), we see that the line covers a certain area that we need to find. However, finding funky circle areas usually requires calculus. \nUsing some intuition, we can see that the lune at the top of the area we need to find is the exact required area to remove and place into the bottom crescent. This transformation shifts the area into a perfect rectangle with one side length equal to the height of the blade:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/f/5bf32b77014c96cc25dad2da21ebed714c82c630.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Height} = 3.5\\]\" width=\"94\" height=\"16\" />\nTo find the other side (the horizontal width), we continue the vertical line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/0/ce0bfc603f9a044e308b1a1fdadca917e81ad2dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{CD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" /> downward at its rightmost position. Then, we draw a perpendicular line from the pivot point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> to this extended line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/0/ce0bfc603f9a044e308b1a1fdadca917e81ad2dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{CD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" />, which touches our new location <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. \nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/7/4c71f19552a05ee5e2b31e94aff3fb986884bf31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B&#39;$\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> be the leftmost position of the arm after the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/f/29fa0a9949f018e121b2093216dd5db13d3abef0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> sweep. If we draw a perpendicular line from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/7/4c71f19552a05ee5e2b31e94aff3fb986884bf31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B&#39;$\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> touching line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/5/c65a0b904f8f63efdc2b5abfef3f4f05b2d3f990.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AF}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, we realize that:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/4/2544145ac52b253ba628c4da185032ae5769ecb5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\triangle AFC \\sim \\triangle AB&#39;G \\text{ by SAS}\\]\" width=\"204\" height=\"17\" />\nBecause of the symmetric angles of the sweep, we know that:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/f/3bfb7a404c62f8688cb37edd707c7baccca87649.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle CAF = \\angle B&#39;AG = 60^\\circ\\]\" width=\"187\" height=\"14\" />\nThis makes it a standard <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/6/bb6e337331fe8189483865ae37ce58a33225f6d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30^\\circ\\text{-}60^\\circ\\text{-}90^\\circ$\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" /> triangle. By the ratios of a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/6/bb6e337331fe8189483865ae37ce58a33225f6d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$30^\\circ\\text{-}60^\\circ\\text{-}90^\\circ$\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" /> triangle, the short leg is half the hypotenuse:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/1/5015ea85ece539fd8c91d89da130d349a1644d76.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[FA = AG = 1.5\\]\" width=\"126\" height=\"13\" />\nSince the width spans symmetrically from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/f/c3f0a3e06f325bcdd9d7fdff38539245c4fe0819.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[FG = 3\\]\" width=\"61\" height=\"12\" />\nNow we have our rectangle dimensions, so we just multiply them to find the total area:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/5/1e52e32529c116f418b8995249605fa61ef7526d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{Area} = 3 \\times 3.5 = 10.5\\]\" width=\"168\" height=\"13\" />\nThe answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/9/169b8d69bd74a91e72ec43123070d770633a340a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(C)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>~Randomuser456 (Note: this is what you do if you don't know Cavalieri's Principle)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We just realize that the sector inside and the sector outside have equal areas so we can just imagine it as a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/3/dd3d016017332661d8b36aa76732f486d7c0e87a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3.5 * 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />rectangle,which gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/7/1e75d89ac03b0419969d24039089d685926479a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{10.5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"45\" height=\"25\" /> (C)\n</p><p>~Aarav22\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=39GPTtpMIqI\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=39GPTtpMIqI</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"